    <!-- ACT 1: the crisis at its worst. The object is not named. -->

    <h2 id="afternoon">The Afternoon of December 30, 1903</h2>

    <p>Most people have never heard of the Iroquois Theatre fire. Not because it was small. Because what happened in those twenty minutes is the exact thing every person in a crowd is afraid of, and the story is easier to leave alone.</p>

    <p>At about 3:15 in the afternoon on Wednesday, December 30, 1903, every seat in the Iroquois Theatre in Chicago was sold, plus hundreds of standing tickets at the back. It was the week between Christmas and New Year. The show was the Drury Lane musical <em>Mr. Blue Beard</em>, and the house was full of women and children on a holiday outing.</p>

    <p>The building had opened five weeks earlier. Its playbills and newspaper advertisements carried one reassuring phrase: absolutely fireproof.</p>

    <p>Then a spark from a broken arc lamp caught a muslin curtain above the stage. The lamp operator, William McMullen, had already told the stage managers that the light was too close to the curtain. Nobody had moved it.</p>

    <p>Two men tried to put the fire out. One of them was the theatre's own fireman, William Sallers, who threw the venue's extinguishers at it. Those extinguishers were tin tubes about two feet long holding three pounds of white powder, and the instructions said to hurl the contents at the base of the flames. The fire was forty feet above the stage. The powder fell uselessly to the floor. The stage manager tried to drop the asbestos safety curtain and it jammed partway down, stopped by a light reflector sticking out under the proscenium arch. When a chemist later tested part of that curtain, he found it was mainly wood pulp mixed with asbestos and would have been of no value in a fire.</p>

    <p>An actor named Eddie Foy was in the wings, waiting to go on. He made sure his young son was being held by a stagehand, then walked out in front of a full house and told people not to panic while burning scenery landed on the stage around him. He is the reason a lot of that audience started moving at all.</p>

    <p>Moving was the easy part. Getting out was the problem.</p>

    <p>The audience on the ground floor walked through the foyer and out onto Randolph Street. The people above them could not, because the stairways were already filling with bodies.</p>

    <p>The Iroquois had been built with one broad stairway serving the balcony and the gallery both. Chicago's fire ordinance required separate stairs and exits for each level, and the design simply ignored that. It was a deliberate choice with a marketing reason: patrons in the cheaper seats were supposed to walk past the expensive ones on the way in, so nobody would feel second class. On the way out, everyone met on the same stairs.</p>

    <p>Iron gates that had been closed across the secondary stairways for the performance were still closed. The gates were there to stop patrons from the cheap seats sneaking down to the expensive levels. On the north side of the building there were fire exits hidden behind drapery, and people did find them, and then discovered that the doors used a style of latch most of them had never worked before.</p>

    <p>One of those doors was opened by a saloon keeper named Frank Houseman, a former professional baseball player. He got it open because the icebox in his own kitchen had a similar catch. He had to argue with an usher to do it. A second door was forced by brute strength. A third gave way to a rush of air. Most of them stayed shut.</p>

    <p>Imagine being in the middle of that staircase. You can see the exit. Your body is being pushed toward it by everyone behind you. The door in front of you has a mechanism you do not recognise, in the dark, and the room at your back is already alight.</p>

    <p>The Chicago reporter William Etten wrote down what he saw when the recovery began: "The dead lay literally in heaps, frail women gasped for the last time from mounds of corpses, puny children sighed their lives away, while an eager morbid crowd pressed forward to look on the face of death. It was more horrible than war, more terrifying than pestilence."</p>

    <p>Corpses were stacked ten feet high around some of the blocked exits. Five hundred and seventy-five people died that day. At least thirty more died of their injuries in the weeks afterwards. The final toll is 602, with 250 people injured. It took about fifteen minutes.</p>

    <p>Of the roughly three hundred actors, dancers and stagehands in the building, five died. One of them was an aerialist named Nellie Reed, whose part required her to fly out over the audience on a trolley wire, dropping pink carnations on the crowd. She was waiting above the stage for her entrance when the fire reached her. She fell, and died of burns and internal injuries three days later.</p>

    <h2 id="fireproof">Absolutely Fireproof</h2>

    <p>Here is what turned a fire into the worst theatre disaster in American history. Almost none of it was a secret.</p>

    <p>An editor from <em>Fireproof Magazine</em> toured the Iroquois while it was still under construction and wrote down what he saw: no stage draft shaft, exposed steel, wood trim on everything, and what he called the inadequate provision of exits. A Chicago Fire Department captain named Patrick Jennings walked through the building days before it opened and found no sprinklers, no alarms, no telephones and no water connections. He raised it with the theatre's own fireman, who did not pass it up the chain because he was afraid the owners would have him dismissed. When Jennings reported it to his battalion chief, he was told that nothing could be done, because the theatre already had a fireman.</p>

    <p><em>The New York Tribune</em> would later note that the Iroquois had no automatic sprinkler system even though the law required one.</p>

    <p>The building had no fire alarm box and no telephone. The fire department found out because a stagehand was sent running to the nearest firehouse. Engine 13 pulled an alarm box on the way, at about 3:33 in the afternoon, eighteen minutes after the fire started. The fire escapes on the north side were icy, narrow, and one of them had been installed badly enough that people tripped as they came through the door onto it. The first people who jumped broke the falls of the ones who landed on them.</p>

    <p>Pay attention to this number, because it is the whole story in one line. Six hundred and two people died inside a building that was not short of doors. It had a main entrance onto Randolph Street, and fire exits on the north wall, and fire escapes on the outside of those exits. What it did not have was a door that still worked when several hundred frightened people were pressed into it at once.</p>

    <p>The instinct after a disaster like this is to demand more doors, more signs, more drills, wider stairs and a limit on how many people are allowed inside. All of those things help. None of them answers the question that killed those people, which is this: what happens at the last door when the person at the front cannot work the latch, cannot see, and cannot turn around?</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: the wrong turns. The object is still not named. -->

    <h2 id="before">It Had Happened Before</h2>

    <p>However, the Iroquois was not a freak event. It was a repeat, and the earlier version is worse to read.</p>

    <p>Twenty years earlier, on 16 June 1883, about 1,100 children packed into the Victoria Hall in Sunderland, in the north of England, for a variety show by a travelling entertainer called Alexander Fay. Most of them were between three and fourteen years old. At the end there was an announcement: children holding tickets with certain numbers would get a prize on the way out. At the same moment, performers began handing out gifts from the stage, and the children in the gallery, terrified of missing out, rushed for the stairs. There were almost no adults up there with them.</p>

    <p>At the bottom of that staircase the door opened inward, and it had been bolted so that it stood open only wide enough for one child to pass at a time. The bolt was on the children's side of the door, and the reason for the narrow gap was orderly ticket checking. The children at the front went down. The weight of the children behind them kept coming.</p>

    <p>A survivor, William Codling Jr, wrote about it eleven years later: "Suddenly I felt that I was treading upon someone lying on the stairs and I cried in horror to those behind, 'Keep back, keep back! There's someone down.' It was no use, I passed slowly over and onwards with the mass and before long I passed over others without emotion."</p>

    <p>The caretaker, Frederick Graham, could not pull the pile of children apart. He ran back up a different staircase and diverted about 600 of them out through another exit. Adults below pulled children one at a time through the narrow gap, and eventually a man wrenched the door off its hinges. One hundred and eighty-three children died of compressive asphyxia. A week later the <em>British Medical Journal</em> published the medical findings in detail.</p>

    <p>Nobody was ever prosecuted. The person who bolted that door was never identified.</p>

    <p>But this is where the story turns, because Britain did something the United States had not yet done. The outcry forced an inquiry, and the inquiry led to a law requiring public entertainment venues to have a minimum number of emergency exits that opened outward. Nine years later, in 1892, a man named Robert Alexander Briggs patented a bolt with a horizontal bar across it, built so that a body pressing against the bar would release the door. He had been moved to invent it by the Sunderland disaster.</p>

    <p>Britain's buildings changed. The idea did not travel. Eleven years later, in Chicago, the same failure killed 602 people who had never heard of Sunderland.</p>

    <h2 id="lesson">The Lessons Everyone Wrote Down Wrong</h2>

    <p>After every one of these fires, the same list of fixes went around. More exits. Wider stairs. More signs and more drills. Limits on how many people can be inside. A rule that the exits must be kept openable from the inside during a performance. Every item on that list is sensible, and every item depends on somebody behaving correctly at the worst possible moment.</p>

    <p>Here is how it kept failing.</p>

    <p>Thirty-nine years after the Iroquois, in Boston, a nightclub called the Cocoanut Grove filled to more than twice its legal capacity on the Saturday after Thanksgiving. The decoration was tropical: palm trees, rattan, heavy draperies, dark blue satin canopies across the ceiling. The exits had been locked to stop people walking out without paying. One emergency exit had been bricked up entirely. Others were concealed behind drapery. The air conditioning had been filled with methyl chloride, a flammable gas, because Freon was rationed during the war. That detail ties this story straight into another one on this site: the same family of chemicals, introduced to make kitchens and clubs safer, went on to tear a hole in the ozone layer. You can read that half of it in <a href="/posts/cold-chain-freon-refrigeration">the story of the refrigerator</a>.</p>

    <p>The fire started in the basement lounge. Four hundred and ninety-two people died. The laws that followed in Massachusetts are the ones you still live inside: emergency exits must be openable from the inside without a key, revolving doors cannot be the only way out, and flammable decorations are banned in places of assembly.</p>

    <p>But that is a promise about human behaviour, and promises break. In Paisley, Scotland, on Hogmanay in 1929, children at the Glen Cinema were crushed against padlocked exit doors after a canister of film began to smoke and panic broke out. A police officer broke one padlock. The doors opened inward, so the mass of bodies behind them held them shut anyway. Seventy-one children died, thirty-seven years after the horizontal-bar bolt had been patented by a man who had been inspired by the deaths of 183 children.</p>

    <p>And one earlier American case quietly destroys the simplest version of this story. At the Lake View School in Collinwood, Ohio, in 1908, 172 children, two teachers and one rescuer died in a fire. The building had only two exits, the fire blocked the front one, and the children who ran for the back door piled up in a vestibule narrowed by partitions, packing the doorway tight enough to seal it. Their doors swung outward, the way the rules said doors should. A narrow passage and a crowd were enough. (You will still read that Collinwood's doors opened inward. That is a myth, and Cleveland reporters were debunking it as early as 1938.)</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: the fix arrives, still slightly oblique. -->

    <h2 id="hardware">The Hardware Man Who Was in Chicago That Day</h2>

    <p>Carl Prinzler was born in Indianapolis in 1870 to German immigrant parents. At sixteen he got a job as a sales clerk at a hardware store downtown, Lilly and Stalnaker, and he liked to tell the story of his first important sale for the rest of his life: he sold a washing machine to a woman who had come in for a butter churn. He did not know what the store actually stocked.</p>

    <p>A year later, in 1887, he moved to the Vonnegut Hardware Company a few blocks away on East Washington Street, and he stayed there for more than fifty years. By 1895 he was managing the department that sold art hardware and building materials. He was, in the way that matters here, a man who knew exactly how a door is put together, and exactly what a shopkeeper believes a door is for.</p>

    <p>And on December 30, 1903, Prinzler was in Chicago. He escaped being one of the victims of the Iroquois fire, by luck or by timing, and he went home to Indianapolis with the problem lodged in his head. Not how to teach a crowd to behave. Not how to make inspectors braver or owners more honest. How to make a door that does not need the crowd to be calm.</p>

    <h2 id="neighbor">A Bar at Waist Height</h2>

    <p>Working with his neighbour, an architect named Henry H. DuPont, Prinzler spent years on that problem, and the two of them filed nine patents for door hardware. All nine were built around one idea: a bar across the door, at the height of a waist, doing the work of a handle, a key and an instruction at the same time.</p>

    <p>Push the bar anywhere along its length, and a spring-loaded mechanism releases the latch. If you have ever looked closely at the double doors in a school or a supermarket, this is what you were looking at: the bar pulls a cord inside vertical rods that drop the latches at the top and bottom of the door at the same instant. The door then swings outward, which is the direction the crowd is already moving. A hand, a hip, the weight of a person falling forward. Any of it opens the door. And from the street, the door stays locked.</p>

    <p>That last part is why the device exists at all. Owners had been locking exits for fifty years because an exit that anybody can push open is money walking out of the building. Now they could have both. A door a burglar cannot open from the pavement, and a door a panicking crowd can open without knowing how.</p>

    <p>The name on the product came from the three men who made it: Vonnegut, DuPont and Prinzler. Von Duprin. The Von Duprin Safe Exit Device went into production in Indianapolis around 1908, and it was sold the way hardware was sold then, by a national network of independent sales representatives calling on schools, hospitals, theatres and public buildings one at a time. Prinzler ran the manufacturing side and joined the company's board in 1910.</p>

    <h2 id="compulsory">Sold, Not Compulsory</h2>

    <p>Here is the key insight, and it is uncomfortable for anyone who believes a good invention changes the world by itself.</p>

    <p>For two decades, the exit device was optional. It spread because a salesman showed up and a building owner decided the price was worth it. The first model code that tried to make exit provisions uniform, the National Fire Protection Association's Building Exits Code, was published in 1927, and it was revised again after the Cocoanut Grove fire in 1942, when it became impossible to argue that a locked door in a crowded room is anything other than a mass-casualty device. The rules tightened after every disaster, and the disaster kept happening in the buildings nobody had gotten to yet.</p>

    <p>What the modern code says is stranger than most people expect. It does not tell building owners to be careful. It says the door is not allowed to have any other kind of latch. In the 2018 International Fire Code, swinging doors serving a room or space with an occupant load of 50 or more in an assembly or educational building shall not be provided with a latch or lock other than panic hardware or fire exit hardware. Rooms full of heavy electrical equipment need it too, whatever the crowd, and those doors must swing in the direction people run.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: the reveal. -->

    <h2 id="push">The Bar You Push</h2>

    <p>So let us land this where it belongs. On the object in your hands.</p>

    <p>You pushed one of these today. You pushed one on the way out of a shop, or a hospital, or a school, or a cinema, or the stairwell at work, and your hand or your hip found it without being told, and the door opened, and you did not look at it. There was no key, no button, and no decision to make. That is the entire design. The people who make it call the category panic hardware, which is a strange and brutal phrase for something built to be pressed by a person who is not thinking clearly.</p>

    <p>Here is why that matters. Every fix earlier in this story asked a human being to behave correctly in the worst minute of their life. Check the tickets slowly. Leave the exits usable. Do not sit in the aisle. Wait your turn at the stairs. Stay calm. The bar asks for nothing. The weight of the panic itself does the work.</p>

    <h2 id="changed">What Changed, and What Did Not</h2>

    <p>What has it bought us? Be careful with this one, because the honest answer is narrower than the sales pitch.</p>

    <p>The two deadliest fires in American buildings remain the Iroquois Theatre in 1903 with 602 dead, and the Cocoanut Grove in 1942 with 492. By the count kept in the fire's own history, only the destruction of the World Trade Center killed more people inside a single American building. Since then, the American fires that killed the most people in a crowd have come in the hundreds, not the thousands. 165 people died at the Beverly Hills Supper Club in Kentucky in 1977, in a building with no sprinklers, no alarm system and exits that led into corridors. 100 died at The Station in Rhode Island in 2003, where the footage from that night shows the crowd jammed solid in the front doorway.</p>

    <p>That is not a clean victory, and the exit device is not the only reason for the difference. Sprinklers, alarms, exit signs, occupancy limits and better building materials all did work. But the door that opens under a body is the part of the system standing directly between a crowd and the street, and it is the one part you have touched with your own hands.</p>

    <p>Here is the honest part: the device never reached everywhere. On 27 January 2013, in Santa Maria in southern Brazil, a band's pyrotechnic set light to the acoustic foam on the ceiling of a nightclub called Kiss. The club had one way in and one way out: the front door. About 90 percent of the 242 people who died were killed by smoke, including hydrogen cyanide released by the burning foam. At least 180 bodies were found in the bathrooms, where people had gone looking for a door that led outside. Nine years later, in Malang in Indonesia, police fired tear gas into the stands at a football match, and 135 people died in the crush that followed.</p>

    <p>And where you sleep, none of this applies. Roughly 2,890 Americans died in home fires in 2023, down from about 5,200 in 1980, and no code requires a bar on the door of a house. That improvement came from smoke alarms, safer furniture and fewer people smoking indoors. What the exit device did was narrower and still enormous. It closed off one specific way for a crowd to die, the way that had already killed 183 children in Sunderland, 602 people in Chicago, and 492 people in Boston.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: the lesson -->

    <h2 id="pearl">The Part That Matters</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that matters.</p>

    <p>The Iroquois was not short of doors. What it lacked was a door that still worked when the person at the front of the crowd could not think, could not see, and could not turn around. Every reform before Prinzler's device tried to improve the crowd. Prinzler's device improved the door. One of those things is possible, and the other one is not.</p>

    <p>There is a second lesson here, and it is about the long, dull middle of a fix. The mechanism was patented in the years after the fire and on sale by about 1908. It spread by salesmen for years before any law required it. The first model code arrived in 1927. Enforcement kept getting tighter for another half century. If you had told Carl Prinzler in 1908 that his latches would end up written into the law of fifty states and most of the world, he would probably have just wanted to know whether the next order had shipped. The invention was the exciting part. The boring part is what saved people.</p>

    <p>That pattern repeats in every story on this site, and the engineers of other eras knew it. The men who built London's sewer system sized the pipes for a city that did not exist yet, because they had watched what happens when a system is built for an average day. You can read that one in <a href="/posts/london-sewers-great-stink">the story of the Great Stink</a>.</p>

    <p>And the third thing, which is the reason this story belongs in a series about objects you use without thinking. You will push three of these bars tomorrow. You will not notice one of them. You will not know the name of the man who spent twenty years designing the latch inside it, or the name of the Englishman who patented a version of it eleven years earlier after 183 children died behind a bolted door, or the name of the saloon keeper who got a door open in Chicago in 1903 only because the icebox in his kitchen had a similar catch.</p>

    <p>That is what a solved problem feels like from the inside. It does not feel like an achievement. It feels like a door.</p>

    <p>Here is the question worth carrying out of this one, and it is worth aiming at something you can actually see. Somewhere near you there is a gate, a stairwell, a fire escape or a service door that only works if somebody makes the right decision in the worst minute. You will probably never have to test it. The 602 people in the Iroquois did not know they were testing theirs until the curtain caught.</p>

